function readme(p){return '# Runnable stateless pilot\n\n'+p.note+'\n\n## Prerequisites for owner review\n\n'+p.prerequisites.map(x=>'- '+x.resource+': '+x.action).join('\n')+'\n\nKeep all files together. Requires Python 3, kubectl and local kubeconfig credentials. Review execution-plan.json, pilot-execution-draft.json and blockers; the runner consumes the bound plan, while the separate draft is for inspection. Prepare the namespace, references, security controls and approved non-production data/dependency access. Images must be digest-pinned. The exported deployment uses one replica. No source resources are modified.\n\n1. Run: python3 pilot-runner.py validate --context TARGET --output pilot-validation.json\n2. Review the validation and recovery procedure. Deployment requires a passing validation from the same plan/context within 30 minutes, the exact workload identifier and explicit prerequisite acknowledgment.\n3. Run: python3 pilot-runner.py deploy --context TARGET --validation pilot-validation.json --confirm-workload '+p.workload+' --ack-prerequisites --output pilot-run.json\n4. Import pilot-run.json into Stateless pilot. To refresh an existing run without redeploying: python3 pilot-runner.py observe --context TARGET --receipt pilot-run.json --output pilot-observation.json . Import the observation after its original deploy receipt. It verifies recorded roots, observes rollout/images and repeats the approved HTTP GET; it creates, updates and deletes no Kubernetes resources. Missing/changed/terminating roots stop the probe. This does not replace preflight, Service/Ingress/policy tests or independent acceptance. Save the matching project and original package for recovery. See RECOVERY.md for inspect and separately confirmed cleanup commands.\n\nOutputs are exclusive 0600 journal files; choose new filenames for each run. The deploy command repeats preflight/isolation checks, performs a create-specific server dry-run and creates Services then Deployment using create-only calls. A race cannot overwrite an existing named object. The runner records UIDs after each create, then rollout, owned Pod runtime image IDs and a loopback-only HTTP GET via kubectl port-forward. HTTP redirects, proxies and response-body export are disabled. This smoke probe bypasses normal Service/Ingress/network-policy paths; validate them separately. A runtime imageID does not necessarily equal a multi-platform registry manifest digest.\n\nExisting target Services/controllers/Pods that overlap the pilot labels block deployment. This is an observation, not a lock; coordinate target changes and competing reconcilers throughout the test. Admission webhooks and application startup can have external effects. Only execute in an owner-approved environment. The app never executes the package.\n\nFailures do not trigger automatic rollback. Read RECOVERY.md and keep the partial receipt; uncertain create outcomes require manual inspection using the run label. No configuration values, raw API objects, raw stderr, HTTP bodies or credentials are exported in results. Production gate always remains closed.\n';}
const recovery='# Pilot recovery procedure\n\nCleanup is a separate explicitly invoked operation; failures never trigger automatic deletion or rollback. Keep the source running; this package never changes source traffic.\n\n1. Stop further pilot tests. Review external side effects and any writes made by the application, including startup tasks. Quiesce/fence pilot writers and use the data owner’s approved reconciliation procedure before failback. Deleting Pods or reversing DNS cannot reconcile data.\n2. Read pilot-run.json, including status, created object names/UIDs and uncertain outcomes. A Started or Failed receipt can still represent live resources. For uncertain creates, inspect each planned name with the explicit target context and verify the migration-studio.openai/pilot-run label matches runId. The receipt is historical, not an authorization token.\n3. Verify kube-system UID still matches clusterUID. For every candidate resource, inspect the live UID, run label, owner relationships and current references. If any differs, stop and resolve ownership; do not delete by name alone. Save a reviewed recovery record.\n4. Remove only this run’s Deployment and ClusterIP Services through your approved Kubernetes procedure, using UID deletion preconditions to protect against name reuse. Stop Service traffic and the pilot workload in an owner-approved order. No namespace, shared reference, policy, registry or source resource should be removed. Kubernetes DeleteOptions supports preconditions.uid; use an API client/operator procedure that supplies it.\n5. The runner supports read-only inspection followed by separately confirmed cleanup. Save your project and original package before deploying; keep using the same bound plan for recovery. Run: python3 pilot-runner.py inspect --context TARGET --receipt pilot-run.json --output pilot-inspection.json\n\nReview every UID/version, run label, mismatch and run-labeled Pod/ReplicaSet. Then, only after writer fencing and external-state review, run: python3 pilot-runner.py cleanup --context TARGET --receipt pilot-run.json --inspection pilot-inspection.json --confirm-run RUN_ID --ack-external-state --output pilot-cleanup.json\n\nUse the exact runId from the deploy receipt. Inspection must be less than ten minutes old and unblocked. Cleanup checks all roots before deleting, rechecks cluster/object identity immediately before each request, and sends both UID and resourceVersion preconditions. It requests Service deletion before Deployment deletion with Foreground propagation, respects normal grace/finalizers, and never forces deletion or removes finalizers. Changed objects require another inspection. Inspect again with a fresh output filename after a partial/failed request. A request can succeed even if its response is lost.\n\n6. Verify removal of the intended objects and their owned Pods/ReplicaSets, check for unknown/orphaned creates after interrupted API calls, and validate retained source service and data consistency. Record application/security/data acceptance independently.\n\nReferences: https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubernetes-api/common-definitions/delete-options/ and https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_port-forward/\n';
root.MigrationExecution={settings,plan,evidence,recoveryEvidence,observationEvidence,observationHistory,appendObservation,observationChanges,timeline,lifecycle,readme,recovery};if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=root.MigrationExecution;
